      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/mccrory_arkansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town's name may stem from a cabin dweller named Jennie surrounded by many children, or honor Jennie Edmonds, an early settler, or her husband James Barnes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/mccrory_arkansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first train arrived in 1887, sparking rapid growth as hotels, schools, churches, and mills sprang up to serve settlers and commerce.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/mccrory_arkansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Much of the surrounding area was once swampland, later drained to support agriculture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/mccrory_arkansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Wade McCrory donated 15 acres in 1886, prompting the Iron Mountain Railroad to lay tracks that helped the town incorporate in 1890.",
